1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:06,028
Figure two. Chat three ways.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:21,492
In many agent tools, a conversation belongs to the window you typed it in. Keelson gives you three ways in: the browser, an interactive terminal, and a one-line command you can script. All three reach the same conversation.

3
00:00:22,292 --> 00:00:27,760
Start with the script. Ask for a codename, and add dash dash json.

4
00:00:28,792 --> 00:00:35,540
One envelope comes back. Its mode says the server ran it, and the conversation ID is the row it wrote.

5
00:00:35,892 --> 00:00:54,992
Here's how. None of the three is the app. Each is a client of one local server, which runs the turn with Copilot and writes the conversation, every message, and the agent's session to SQLite. So the next turn picks up the same thread, whichever way it comes in.

6
00:00:56,042 --> 00:01:03,798
So open the browser. The scripted turn is already here. It never lived in the terminal. It lived in the store.

7
00:01:04,232 --> 00:01:07,724
Ask why that name, and the agent answers from the same thread.

8
00:01:09,185 --> 00:01:14,655
Now the terminal. Hand the interactive chat that same ID, straight from the envelope.

9
00:01:15,785 --> 00:01:17,605
Ask for a tagline.

10
00:01:19,485 --> 00:01:23,323
Breakwater again. The agent never lost the thread.

11
00:01:25,165 --> 00:01:29,065
And back in the browser, all three turns sit in one conversation.

12
00:01:31,005 --> 00:01:46,729
Why three? Each is good at something. The browser is for reading and exploring. The terminal keeps you in the shell, and can switch models mid-session. And the script makes a chat a building block, with JSON to pipe and an exit code to branch on.

13
00:01:47,329 --> 00:01:52,797
Three ways in, one store behind them. Everything after this builds on that store.

14
00:01:55,497 --> 00:01:59,541
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.

